Answer: A single-line comment is used to comment only one line. It begins with two forward slashes, //.

```java
// This is a single-line comment
System.out.println("Hello Java");
```

Everything written after // on that line is ignored by the compiler.

Answer: A documentation comment is used to provide information about a class, method, or program. It begins with /** and ends with */.

```java
/**
* This class demonstrates Java comments.
* It explains the purpose of the program.
*/
public class Demo {
}
```

Documentation comments are commonly used with the Javadoc tool to generate documentation.

Answer: Hard-coded data is a value directly written inside the program instead of being received from the user or another external source.

```java
int age = 25;
String name = "Raju";
```

In this example, 25 and "Raju" are hard-coded values.

Answer: The Scanner object reads input from the keyboard and returns the entered value to the program. For example:

```java
Scanner scan = new Scanner(System.in);
int age = scan.nextInt();
```

In this example, the Scanner object reads an integer from the user and stores it in the age variable.

Answer: Call an extra nextLine() after nextInt() and before the actual nextLine() that reads the string.

```java
System.out.println("Enter age:");
int age = scan.nextInt();
scan.nextLine();
System.out.println("Enter full name:");
String fullName = scan.nextLine();
```

The first nextLine() consumes the leftover newline character. The second nextLine() reads the actual full name.

Answer: If nextLine() is used first and nextInt() is used afterward, the problem does not occur because nextLine() consumes the complete input line, including the Enter character. Example:

```java
System.out.println("Enter full name:");
String fullName = scan.nextLine();
System.out.println("Enter age:");
int age = scan.nextInt();
```

This order works without adding an extra nextLine().

