Answer: BufferedReader is used to efficiently read text from a character-input stream by buffering data in memory. It drastically improves performance by reducing the number of costly direct I/O operations. Its primary advantage is the ability to read input line-by-line using the readLine() method. Example:

```java
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;
import java.io.IOException;
public class Main {
public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
// System.in reads bytes, InputStreamReader converts bytes to characters
BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));
System.out.print("Enter your name: ");
String name = reader.readLine(); // Reads the whole line
System.out.println("Hello, " + name);
}
}
```
